JSON.stringify()can convert all JavaScript values thatJSON supports.

A replacer arraycan choose which properties to include.

The resulting JSON contains only the selected properties.

A replacer functioncan modify values before they are converted.

The optionalspaceparameter makes JSON easier to read.

The valuenullselects NO replacer.

The value1indents each level with one space.

Some JavaScript values cannot be represented in JSON.

The JSON.stringify() function will convert Date objects into strings.

You can convert the string back into a date object at the receiver.

When storing data, the data has to be a certain format, and regardless of where you choose to store it,textis always one of the legal formats.

JSON makes it possible to store JavaScript objects as text.

Calling JSON.stringify() twice, adds escaped quotation marks.

JSON.stringify() cannot convert objects that contain circular references.

### Code Snippets

```
JSON.stringify(value, replacer, space)

---

// Create a JavaScript object
const person = {
name: "John",
age: 30,
city: "New York"
};
// Convert the object to a JSON text
const text = JSON.stringify(person);

---

// Create a JavaScript array
const cars = ["Ford", "Volvo", "BMW"];
// Convert the array to a JSON text
const text = JSON.stringify(cars);

---

JSON.stringify("John");
JSON.stringify(42);
JSON.stringify(false);
JSON.stringify(Boolean(0));
JSON.stringify(true);
JSON.stringify(Boolean(1));
JSON.stringify(undefined);
// These returns null
JSON.stringify(null);
JSON.stringify(NaN);
JSON.stringify(Infinity);

---

const person = {
name: "John",
age: 30,
city: "New York"
};
let text = JSON.stringify(person, ["name", "city"]);

---

const person = {
name: "John",
age: 30
};
const text = JSON.stringify(person,
function(key, value) {
if (key == "age") {
return value + 1;
}
return value;
});

---

const person = {
name: "John",
age: 30,
city: "New York"
};
let text = JSON.stringify(person, null, 1);

---

const person = {
name: "John",
greet: function() {},
age: undefined
};
let text = JSON.stringify(person);

---

const person = {
name: "John",
greet: NAN,
age: Infinity
};
let text = JSON.stringify(person);

---

// JavaScript Array
const cars = ["Ford", "Volvo", function() {}, undefined, NaN, Infinity];
// Convert the array to JSON
let text = JSON.stringify(cars);

---

const person = {name:"John", today:date, city:"New York"};
let text = JSON.stringify(person);

---

// Store data:
const myObj = {name: "John", age: 31, city: "New York"};
const myJSON = JSON.stringify(myObj);
localStorage.setItem("testJSON", myJSON);
// Retrieve data:
let text = localStorage.getItem("testJSON");
let obj = JSON.parse(text);

---

const person = {name:"John"};
const text = JSON.stringify(person);
const textAgain = JSON.stringify(text);

---

const person = {};
person.self = person;
JSON.stringify(person);
```
